    <p>The three-way tie at four wins is historically significant. <strong>John Williams</strong> accumulated his wins across the blockbuster era (1975&ndash;1993), defining the sound of tentpole cinema. <strong>John Barry</strong> spanned the widest range &mdash; 24 years from <em>Born Free</em> to <em>Dances with Wolves</em> &mdash; bridging the 1960s and the late 1980s. <strong>Ludwig G&ouml;ransson</strong>, the most recent entrant to this club, achieved four wins in just eight years, the fastest accumulation in the dataset.</p>

    <p>Notably, <strong>Hans Zimmer's</strong> 27-year gap between wins (1994 to 2021) represents the longest drought-to-comeback arc in the category's history, underscoring both his longevity and the Academy's shifting tastes.</p>
