      <h2>Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>
    </div>

    <ul class="milestone-list">
      <li>
        <div class="ml-year">1934</div>
        <div class="ml-title">The First Winner</div>
        <div class="ml-desc">Louis Silvers wins the inaugural Best Original Score Oscar for <em>One Night of Love</em> at the 6th Academy Awards, establishing the category that would become one of the ceremony's most enduring honors.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="ml-year">1935</div>
        <div class="ml-title">Max Steiner: The First Icon</div>
        <div class="ml-desc">Max Steiner wins for <em>The Informer</em>, beginning a career that would earn him three Oscars and establish the template for the Hollywood film composer as a recognized artistic force.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="ml-year">1950&ndash;1951</div>
        <div class="ml-title">Franz Waxman's Back-to-Back Wins</div>
        <div class="ml-desc">Franz Waxman wins consecutively for <em>Sunset Boulevard</em> and <em>A Place in the Sun</em> &mdash; the first back-to-back wins in category history. Only Gustavo Santaolalla (2005&ndash;2006) and Ludwig G&ouml;ransson (2023&ndash;2024, per the data) have since matched this feat.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="ml-year">1975 &amp; 1977</div>
        <div class="ml-title">John Williams Launches the Blockbuster Score</div>
        <div class="ml-desc">Williams wins for <em>Jaws</em> and <em>Star Wars</em> within three years, redefining what a film score could mean at the box office and in popular culture. His four total wins would stand as a record for decades.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="ml-year">1989&ndash;1992</div>
        <div class="ml-title">Alan Menken's Disney Renaissance</div>
        <div class="ml-desc">Menken wins three out of four ceremonies (<em>The Little Mermaid</em>, <em>Beauty and the Beast</em>, <em>Aladdin</em>), driving the Disney animated musical renaissance and dominating the early 1990s scoring landscape.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="ml-year">1994 &rarr; 2021</div>
        <div class="ml-title">Hans Zimmer: The 27-Year Comeback</div>
        <div class="ml-desc">Hans Zimmer wins for <em>The Lion King</em> in 1994, then waits 27 years before his second win for <em>Dune</em> in 2021 &mdash; the longest gap between wins for any composer in the dataset. In the intervening decades, he became one of the most influential film composers alive.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="ml-year">2015</div>
        <div class="ml-title">Ennio Morricone's Belated Honor</div>
        <div class="ml-desc">After a legendary career spanning decades and a 2007 Honorary Oscar, Ennio Morricone wins his first competitive Best Original Score for <em>The Hateful Eight</em> at age 87.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="ml-year">2019</div>
        <div class="ml-title">Hildur Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir Breaks the Gender Barrier</div>
        <div class="ml-desc">Hildur Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir becomes the first solo woman to win Best Original Score for <em>Joker</em>, a milestone in a category that had been almost exclusively male for 85 years.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="ml-year">2018&ndash;2026</div>
        <div class="ml-title">Ludwig G&ouml;ransson's Rapid Ascent</div>
        <div class="ml-desc">Starting with <em>Black Panther</em> in 2018, G&ouml;ransson accumulates four wins in just eight years &mdash; the fastest rise to the top tier in the award's history. His 2026 win for <em>Sinners</em> ties him with Williams and Barry at the dataset maximum.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="ml-year">2025</div>
        <div class="ml-title">The Most Recent First-Time Winner</div>
        <div class="ml-desc">Daniel Blumberg wins for <em>The Brutalist</em>, the most recent debut winner in the dataset, continuing the category's tradition of recognizing fresh voices alongside established masters.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
